        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(cron): keep valid env vars when a sibling env value is non-string",
        "body": "Closes #105433\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where users creating or updating a cron command job with `env` that includes any non-string value (for example `env: { FOO: \"bar\", DEBUG: true }`) would silently lose the entire `env` block during payload normalization, including the valid string entries.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nCron payload normalization now drops only invalid env keys/values and keeps the remaining string entries, matching the existing command payload SQLite codec filter behavior. If no valid entries remain, `env` is still removed.\n\n## User Impact\n\nCommand cron jobs keep their valid environment variables when one sibling value is the wrong type, instead of wiping the whole `env` map.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Focused tests in `src/cron/normalize.test.ts` cover mixed valid/invalid env and all-invalid env.\n- Local proof (Testbox/Crabbox binary unavailable in this environment): `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/cron/normalize.test.ts` → 62 passed.\n\n\nMade with [Cursor](https://cursor.com)",
